Development and Evaluation of an AI-Based Interview Learning Support System
Abstract
Preparing for technical job interviews requires not only domain knowledge but also the
ability to communicate answers clearly, structure responses effectively, and explain
problem-solving approaches. Although recent AI-based interview coaching systems and
large language models can generate interview questions and feedback, most focus on
conversation and question generation rather than measuring learning outcomes. As a
result, it remains unclear whether AI-generated feedback actually helps students improve
their interview responses.
This research proposes the development of a lightweight AI-based interview coaching
prototype that provides structured rubric-based feedback and radar-chart visualization.
The primary objective is to investigate whether structured AI-generated feedback helps
students improve the quality of their interview responses and better understand how to
revise their answers. The system evaluates responses using predefined criteria including
relevance, structure, clarity, technical accuracy, and communication.
The study adopts a design-and-evaluation methodology in which participants answer
interview questions, receive structured feedback, revise their responses, and submit
improved answers. Learning improvement is measured through changes in rubric scores
between initial and revised responses, while confidence questionnaires are used as a
secondary measure. The research contributes to AI-assisted educational technology by
providing a reproducible and interpretable framework for evaluating learning during
interview preparation.
