1. Introduction
Interview preparation is a critical component of student career development, particularly
for those pursuing technical roles in the software industry. Technical interviews require
not only knowledge of concepts but also the ability to clearly explain problem-solving
approaches, structure responses, and communicate effectively under time constraints.
Success in these interviews depends on both technical competence and communication
skills, making interview preparation an essential aspect of employability.
General-purpose LLMs such as ChatGPT can generate interview questions and provide
feedback when users supply an appropriate prompt, job description, and interview
context. However, these systems are primarily designed for conversational interaction
rather than educational evaluation. Feedback varies depending on prompt wording,
conversation history, and user interaction, making it difficult to provide standardized
assessment or measure learning across different users and sessions. Furthermore,
ChatGPT does not inherently provide structured rubric-based scoring, radar-chart
visualization, or mechanisms for evaluating learning improvement through response
revision. Therefore, the contribution of this research is not the development of another
interview chatbot, but the design and evaluation of a structured learning support
prototype that enables consistent feedback and measurable learning outcomes.
Recent advances in artificial intelligence, particularly large language models (LLMs), have
enabled the development of systems capable of generating realistic interview questions
and evaluating user responses. However, existing research largely focuses on the
functionality of these systems, such as question generation, conversational interaction,
and personalization, rather than investigating how AI-generated feedback supports
learning. Consequently, there is limited evidence regarding whether structured AI
feedback helps students improve the quality of their interview responses or better
understand effective communication strategies.
To address this gap, this research investigates the use of structured AI-generated feedback
as a learning support mechanism for technical interview preparation. A lightweight
prototype system is developed to provide rubric-based evaluation across multiple
dimensions, including relevance, structure, clarity, technical accuracy, and
communication. The feedback is presented through numerical scores, written
recommendations, and radar-chart visualization to improve interpretability and support
reflection.
The primary objective of this study is not to evaluate the technical capabilities of the AI
system itself, but to investigate whether structured AI-generated feedback supports
learning during technical interview preparation. Specifically, the research examines
whether students can improve the quality of their interview responses after receiving
