2. Literature Review
Recent advancements in Artificial Intelligence, particularly Large Language Models
(LLMs), have significantly transformed interview preparation systems by enabling
dynamic question generation, personalized feedback, and real-time interaction. Existing
research demonstrates the potential of AI-driven interview coaching systems to simulate
realistic interview environments and provide individualized support. This section reviews
existing work in AI-driven interview coaching, focusing on LLM-based systems,
multimodal frameworks, and adaptive feedback mechanisms while identifying
limitations that motivate the present study.
LLM-Based Interview Systems
Recent studies have demonstrated that large language models (LLMs) can generate
realistic interview questions and provide personalized feedback. Behzadan et al. (2025)
showed that LLM-generated feedback can support interview preparation and is perceived
positively by users, while Deshpande and Chaudhari (2026) highlighted the use of prompt
engineering to improve personalization and response quality. Similarly, Rupareliya and
Bangoria (2026) demonstrated that AI-based career coaching systems can increase user
engagement through adaptive conversational interactions.
However, these studies primarily evaluate the capability of LLMs to generate questions
and feedback rather than investigating whether the feedback leads to measurable learning
improvement. Feedback is typically presented as conversational text without
standardized rubrics or consistent evaluation criteria, making it difficult to compare
learner performance over time or determine whether students actually improve after
receiving AI-generated feedback.
Full-Stack AI Interview Platforms
Kulkarni et al. (2025) proposed a full-stack AI interview coaching platform integrating
generative AI with a modern web application. The system provides role-specific interview
questions, résumé support, and AI-generated feedback, demonstrating the practical
feasibility of AI-assisted interview preparation.
Despite its practical implementation, the study places greater emphasis on application
functionality than on educational evaluation. Improvements are primarily reported
through user experience and confidence rather than objective evidence of learning, and
the evaluation process lacks a structured rubric for measuring changes in response quality
after feedback.
2
Multimodal Interview Analysis Systems
Vinodhini et al. (2025) and Yamini et al. (2024) extended interview coaching by
incorporating multimodal analysis, including speech, facial expressions, and emotion
recognition. These approaches provide richer assessment of interview behaviour and
more realistic interview simulations.
Although multimodal systems offer comprehensive analysis, they introduce additional
computational complexity while providing limited evidence that the extra modalities
improve learning outcomes. Furthermore, the evaluation criteria are often difficult for
learners to interpret, reducing the educational usefulness of the generated feedback.
Adaptive and Conversational AI Systems
Deshmukh et al. (2026), Madanachitran et al. (2025), and the NexInterview platform
employ conversational AI to generate adaptive interview questions and personalized
follow-up interactions. These systems improve realism and user engagement by adjusting
interview difficulty according to user responses.
However, adaptive questioning alone does not demonstrate whether learners improve
their interview skills. Most studies emphasize conversational realism and interaction
quality, while providing limited evidence regarding structured assessment, response
revision, or measurable learning gains resulting from AI-generated feedback.
Evaluation rubrics
Educational assessment research has consistently demonstrated that analytic rubrics
improve the consistency, transparency, and interpretability of performance evaluation.
Widely used frameworks such as the AAC&U VALUE Rubrics and oral communication
assessment rubrics evaluate learners across multiple predefined criteria, enabling more
reliable assessment and clearer identification of strengths and areas for improvement.
These principles motivate the structured rubric-based evaluation framework adopted in
this study.
The literature demonstrates that AI has become increasingly effective at generating
interview questions, simulating conversations, and providing personalized feedback.
However, comparatively little research investigates AI as a learning support tool by
measuring whether structured feedback enables students to improve the quality of their
interview responses after revision. In addition, existing studies rarely combine
standardized rubric-based evaluation, interpretable visualization, and learning
measurement within a single educational framework. These limitations motivate the
present study, which focuses on evaluating learning improvement rather than developing
another interview coaching application.
