Existing AI interview preparation systems primarily focus on question generation,
conversational realism, personalization, and interview simulation. While these systems
successfully automate interview practice, relatively few studies have investigated whether
structured AI-generated feedback leads to measurable improvements in the quality of
interview responses. Most existing research evaluates the ability of AI systems to generate
questions and provide feedback rather than examining whether learners can use that
feedback to improve their answers.
Another limitation is that feedback is often presented in free-text form without clearly
defined evaluation criteria. As a result, learners may find it difficult to identify specific
strengths and weaknesses or understand how their responses improve over time. In
addition, few studies provide interpretable feedback mechanisms that allow users to
compare performance across multiple evaluation dimensions or across repeated
interview attempts.
Therefore, there is a need to investigate whether structured AI-generated feedback can
support learning during interview preparation. In particular, few studies have measured
learning improvement by comparing initial and revised interview responses using
standardized evaluation criteria. This study addresses that gap by examining how
structured rubric-based feedback influences response quality, learner understanding, and
revision performance during technical interview preparation.
