4. Novelty and Research Contribution
The novelty of this research is not the use of AI itself, but the use of structured AI-
generated feedback as a learning intervention for interview preparation. Although
general-purpose LLMs can provide interview coaching, they are not specifically designed
to support standardized evaluation, learning measurement, or progress tracking.
This study investigates whether structured AI-generated feedback helps learners improve
the quality of their interview responses through revision and reflection. The proposed
approach combines:
• Structured rubric-based evaluation using predefined assessment criteria
• Radar-chart visualization for interpretable multi-dimensional feedback
• Response revision analysis to measure learning improvement between initial and
revised answers
• Confidence measurement through pre–post questionnaires as a secondary
outcome
• Controlled prompting procedures to improve consistency and reproducibility
This research evaluates whether structured rubric-based AI feedback improves interview
learning by comparing students' initial and revised responses. The study contributes
empirical evidence regarding the educational value of structured AI feedback and
investigates how visualization techniques such as radar charts influence learners'
understanding of their performance.
