5. Research Questions and Alignment
To address the identified research gap, this study investigates whether structured AI-
generated feedback can support learning during technical interview preparation. The
research focuses on measuring improvements in interview-response quality and
understanding how learners use feedback to revise their answers.
Research Questions
RQ1: Does structured AI-generated feedback improve the quality of students’ interview
responses after revision?
RQ2: Which response dimensions improve the most after feedback, including relevance,
structure, clarity, technical accuracy, and communication?
RQ3: How do students perceive the usefulness of structured AI feedback for
understanding and improving their interview responses?
Hypothesis:
H1: Students will achieve significantly higher rubric scores after revising responses using
structured AI-generated feedback.
H2: Radar-chart visualization will improve students' understanding of their strengths and
weaknesses compared with text-only feedback.
H3: Participants will report increased confidence after completing structured AI-assisted
interview practice.
6. System Architecture
The proposed system is implemented as a lightweight web-based prototype consisting of
four main components: a user interface, an application server, an AI processing module,
and a database. The user interface allows participants to answer interview questions,
receive structured feedback, and view radar-chart visualizations. The application server
communicates with the AI model through the Groq API, applies a predefined evaluation
prompt and rubric, and returns structured scores and feedback. Interview responses,
evaluation results, and questionnaire data are stored in a PostgreSQL database for
subsequent analysis. The architecture is intentionally simple because the primary
objective of this research is to evaluate the educational effectiveness of structured AI-
generated feedback rather than develop a production-ready interview coaching platform.
7. System Design
The proposed AI Interview Coach system is designed to provide an interactive and
intelligent interview preparation environment through AI-powered response evaluation.
The system combines frontend interaction, authentication, AI integration, backend
6
processing, and database management to deliver personalized interview coaching. Rather
than functioning solely as a conversational chatbot, the system emphasizes educational
effectiveness by providing interpretable and measurable feedback that supports long-
term learning.
During each interview session, participants are presented with a technical interview
question and submit an initial text-based response through the web interface. The
response is sent to an AI model via the Groq API together with a predefined evaluation
prompt and rubric. The AI evaluates the response across five dimensions: relevance,
structure, clarity, technical accuracy, and communication. Each dimension is assigned a
score on a five-point scale, accompanied by written feedback explaining areas for
improvement.
The evaluation results are presented to participants as numerical scores, written feedback,
and a radar-chart visualization. Using this feedback, participants revise their original
response and submit an improved version. The revised response is then evaluated using
the same rubric, enabling comparison between the initial and revised scores. The
improvement in scores represents the primary measure of learning gain in this study.
In addition to response evaluation, participants complete brief pre- and post-session
confidence questionnaires. Confidence is treated as a secondary outcome, while
improvement in interview-response quality remains the primary focus of the research.
The collected responses, scores, and questionnaire results are stored in a PostgreSQL
database for subsequent analysis.
The prototype is intentionally limited to the functionality required for the educational
experiment. Features such as user authentication, voice input, long-term progress
tracking, and multi-session history are outside the scope of the current study.
