8. Methodology
Research Design
This research adopts a design-and-evaluation methodology to investigate whether
structured AI-generated feedback can support learning during technical interview
preparation. A lightweight AI-based interview coaching prototype will be developed to
provide interview questions, rubric-based feedback, and performance visualization. The
primary focus of the study is not the technical implementation of the system itself, but the
evaluation of how learners use and benefit from structured feedback.
The study is conducted in two phases. The first phase involves the development of a
prototype interview coaching system using prompt engineering and predefined
evaluation rubrics. The second phase focuses on empirical evaluation, where participants
complete interview tasks, receive structured AI-generated feedback, revise their
responses, and submit improved answers. Changes between initial and revised responses
are used to measure learning improvement.
This approach enables the research to examine whether structured feedback helps
students improve response quality, understand their strengths and weaknesses, and
develop more effective interview communication skills. By focusing on measurable
learning outcomes rather than system functionality alone, the study contributes to the
understanding of how AI-generated feedback can be used as a learning support tool in
interview preparation.
Participants and Experimental Design
Twenty university students will participate in the study. Each participant will complete
one interview session consisting of five technical interview questions. For each question,
participants will first submit an initial response. The system will then generate structured
AI feedback together with rubric-based scores and radar-chart visualization. Participants
will review the feedback and revise their responses accordingly. The revised responses
will then be re-evaluated using the same rubric.
This process allows the study to measure learning improvement by comparing initial and
revised responses. At the beginning and end of the session, participants will complete a
confidence questionnaire. After completing the interview session, participants will also
provide short written reflections describing what they learned from the feedback and how
they revised their answers.
8
Prompt Engineering Strategy
The system uses a fixed prompt framework to ensure consistency across interview
sessions and participants. Each prompt includes standardized interviewer instructions,
interview context, and predefined evaluation criteria. Responses are assessed using a
rubric covering relevance, structure, clarity, technical accuracy, and communication. To
improve reproducibility, the same AI model, prompt template, evaluation rubric, and
model settings (e.g., temperature) are used throughout the study. The model returns
structured outputs consisting of numerical scores and explanatory feedback, which are
subsequently used for radar-chart visualization and learning analysis.
System Workflow
The system operates through a learning-oriented workflow designed to evaluate the
effectiveness of structured AI-generated feedback. Participants are presented with
interview questions and first submit an initial response. The response is then evaluated
by the AI using a predefined rubric covering relevance, structure, clarity, technical
accuracy, and communication.
Based on this evaluation, the system provides structured feedback consisting of numerical
scores, written recommendations, and radar-chart visualization. Participants review the
feedback and revise their original response accordingly. The revised response is then re-
evaluated using the same rubric and evaluation procedure.
Learning improvement is measured by comparing rubric scores between the initial and
revised responses. Changes across individual evaluation dimensions are analyzed to
identify areas of improvement. In addition, participants complete a questionnaire
regarding the usefulness of the feedback and their confidence in interview preparation.
This workflow enables the study to examine whether structured AI-generated feedback
helps learners understand weaknesses, revise responses, and improve interview
performance.
Evaluation Methodology
The proposed system evaluates learning improvement using a structured AI-assisted
assessment framework. Participant responses are evaluated using a fixed prompt
template, predefined scoring rubric, and controlled model settings to improve
consistency across evaluations. Responses are assessed across five dimensions: relevance,
structure, clarity, technical accuracy, and communication. Each dimension is scored on a
five-point scale, where higher scores indicate better performance.
For each interview question, participants first submit an initial response and then receive
structured AI-generated feedback consisting of numerical scores, written
recommendations, and radar-chart visualization. Participants subsequently revise their
responses based on the feedback and submit an improved version. The revised response
is evaluated using the same rubric and scoring procedure.
9
Learning improvement is measured by comparing scores between the initial and revised
responses across all evaluation dimensions. Radar charts are used to visualize
performance profiles and highlight areas of improvement. In addition, participants
complete pre–post questionnaires to evaluate their perceptions of feedback usefulness
and changes in interview confidence. Confidence is treated as a secondary outcome, while
the primary outcome of the study is improvement in interview-response quality following
structured feedback.
To improve reproducibility, all participants receive the same interview questions,
evaluation rubric, scoring instructions, and model configuration. The use of controlled
prompts and structured outputs reduces variability in AI-generated evaluations and
supports meaningful comparison across participants.
To verify the reliability of the AI-generated scores, a subset of participant responses will
be independently evaluated by an experienced technology professional with more than 30
years of industry experience using the same evaluation rubric. The agreement between
the human evaluator and the AI-generated scores will be analyzed to assess the
consistency and reliability of the AI-based evaluation.
