B. User Confidence Evaluation
User confidence is evaluated as a secondary outcome independently from AI-based
performance scoring through pre–post questionnaires administered before and after
using the system. While the primary objective of this research is to measure learning
improvement through changes in interview-response quality, confidence measures are
included to examine whether structured feedback also influences interview readiness and
self-perception. Participants rate a series of statements using a five-point Likert scale,
where 1 indicates strongly disagree and 5 indicates strongly agree. The questionnaire
includes statements such as: “I feel confident answering interview questions,” “I know
how to structure my responses,” “I understand my areas for improvement,” and “I feel
more prepared for interviews.” These measures are intended to capture changes in
interview readiness and self-perceived confidence.
11
Confidence improvement will be calculated as:
Confidence Gain = PostConfidence − PreConfidence (2)
where PreConfidence represents the average questionnaire score before interview
practice and PostConfidence represents the average score after completing the interview
sessions. Positive values indicate increased confidence following practice, values equal to
zero indicate no change, and negative values indicate reduced confidence. Separating
subjective confidence measures from objective AI-generated scores improves
interpretability and allows different aspects of learning outcomes to be analyzed
independently.
C. Consistency and Reproducibility Controls
Because generative AI models may produce varying outputs across repeated executions,
this study incorporates several controls to improve consistency and reproducibility.
Controlled prompting techniques are used, including fixed evaluation templates,
predefined scoring rubrics, low-temperature inference settings, and standardized
interview scenarios for all participants. Structured output formats such as JSON are also
used to ensure consistent score generation and easier downstream processing.
These controls are designed to reduce variability introduced by language models and
improve comparability across users and experimental sessions. By separating objective
AI-based assessment from subjective confidence evaluation, the proposed framework
seeks to reduce evaluation bias while maintaining interpretability and reproducibility of
results.
D. Score Calibration
To further reduce scoring variability, the AI evaluation system employs predefined rubrics
and fixed evaluation prompts. A benchmark dataset consisting of sample interview
responses representing different quality levels will be created. Prior to participant
evaluation, the AI scoring system will be tested repeatedly using these benchmark
responses to verify whether scores remain consistent across multiple evaluations.
The benchmark responses serve as reference anchors for the scoring scale and provide a
mechanism for calibrating AI-generated evaluations. This calibration process helps
ensure that scores assigned by the system remain stable and comparable across different
users and interview sessions.
