Reproducibility and Experimental Control
Since generative AI outputs may vary across executions, this study adopts several
experimental controls to improve reproducibility. All participants receive identical
system prompts, evaluation rubrics, and scoring instructions to ensure consistency in
question generation and evaluation. The language model configuration is also fixed by
using the same model version, temperature settings, and token limits throughout the
study.
Furthermore, the evaluation pipeline separates question generation from response
scoring. Question generation follows the sequence: Role → Prompt → Question, while
evaluation follows the sequence: Response → Rubric → Score. All prompts, outputs, and
evaluation results are logged and stored for later analysis. These controls reduce variation
caused by conversational drift and enable meaningful comparison across participants and
interview sessions.
Data Analysis
The collected data will be analyzed using both quantitative and qualitative methods.
Quantitative analysis will focus on measuring learning improvement by comparing rubric
scores between participants’ initial responses and revised responses after receiving AI-
generated feedback. Improvements in relevance, structure, clarity, technical accuracy,
and communication will be examined to identify which dimensions benefit most from
structured feedback.
Qualitative data obtained from participant questionnaires and open-ended feedback will
be analyzed thematically to understand user perceptions of feedback usefulness,
strengths of the system, and areas requiring improvement.
