predefined rubric, revise their response, and submit an improved version. Initial and
revised responses are evaluated using the same rubric covering relevance, structure,
clarity, technical accuracy, and communication. Learning improvement is measured by
comparing the rubric scores before and after revision.
Radar-chart visualization is used to present changes across the evaluation dimensions
and help participants interpret their strengths and weaknesses. Participants also
complete pre- and post-session questionnaires to evaluate perceived confidence and the
usefulness of the feedback. Confidence is treated as a secondary outcome, while
improvement in interview-response quality is the primary measure of educational
effectiveness.
To verify the reliability of AI-generated scores, a subset of participant responses will be
independently evaluated by an experienced technology professional with more than 30
years of industry experience using the same evaluation rubric. Agreement between the
human and AI evaluations will be analyzed to assess the consistency of the scoring process.
10. Expected Contribution
This research contributes to the field of AI-assisted educational technology by
investigating whether structured AI-generated feedback can improve student learning
during interview preparation. Unlike existing interview coaching tools that primarily
provide conversational feedback, the proposed framework focuses on helping students
understand, revise, and improve their responses through rubric-based evaluation and
visual feedback.
15
The study introduces a structured evaluation framework that assesses interview
responses across multiple dimensions, including relevance, structure, clarity, technical
accuracy, and communication. By combining standardized scoring with radar-chart
visualization, the system aims to make feedback more interpretable and actionable for
learners.
A further contribution of this research is the use of revision-based learning assessment.
By comparing students’ initial and revised responses after receiving AI-generated
feedback, the study provides evidence of learning improvement rather than relying solely
on user satisfaction or confidence measures.
The research also contributes to understanding how students perceive and utilize AI-
generated feedback during interview preparation. The findings may provide insights into
the design of future AI-supported learning systems that emphasize feedback quality,
interpretability, and measurable learning outcomes.
